# Spec-suite modules that ARE valid and that c/lib/wasm refuses -- the ratchet
# for `make test-wasm`.  A new entry is a FAILURE; an entry here is a debt with
# a name.
#
# WHY THERE IS A LIST AT ALL, AND WHY IT IS NOT A BUG LIST.  The corpus is the
# LIVE spec suite, which is several proposals ahead of the MVP this decoder
# implements, so a module can be valid WebAssembly today and correctly refused
# by an MVP validator.  Everything below was read, and every one of the 51 is
# one of four post-MVP features:
#
#   multiple memories       the multi-memory proposal.  MVP allows one memory,
#                           counting imports, and refusing a second is the MVP
#                           rule rather than a limitation of this code.
#   multiple tables         same, for tables (reference types).
#   zero byte expected      memory.size / memory.grow / memory.copy carrying a
#                           NON-ZERO memory index.  In MVP that byte is
#                           reserved and must be 0x00; multi-memory redefines
#                           it as an index.  Accepting a non-zero value would
#                           silently address the wrong memory, which is exactly
#                           the "present and wrong" failure this project refuses.
#   constant expression required / unknown index
#                           extended-const (i32.add inside an initialiser) and
#                           the 2.0/GC relaxation that lets an initialiser read
#                           a global DEFINED in the same module.  MVP restricts
#                           constant expressions to one const instruction or a
#                           global.get of an IMPORTED immutable global.
#
# A FIFTH CATEGORY USED TO BE HERE AND IT WAS A BUG IN THIS DECODER, NOT A
# DEBT: seven `malformed value type` entries for elem.wast's `(table 1 (ref
# func) (ref.func 0))`.  The byte is 0x40 -- the GC proposal's
# table-with-initialiser form, not an element type at all -- and reading it as
# an element type produced a REJECTION of a well-formed module, which this
# ratchet then recorded as expected.  A baseline is where a wrong verdict goes
# to look permanent.  wasm_parse.c now refuses 0x40, 0x63/0x64 and the GC
# heap-type shorthands BY NAME, so those cases moved to the out-of-scope
# column where they can be counted rather than excused.
#
# Origins are `file.wast:LINE` at the testsuite revision in
# tools/wasm_revision.txt.  Both corpus modes appear here: wast2json and the
# no-dependency binary-only extractor cover different halves of the suite, so
# the file is the UNION and each mode leaves the other's entries unmatched --
# the harness reports that as a COUNT, not as "stale", because calling a live
# entry stale is how one gets deleted.
align0.wast:3	multiple memories
binary0.wast:16	multiple memories
binary0.wast:2	multiple memories
binary0.wast:8	multiple memories
data.wast:178	constant expression required
data.wast:183	constant expression required
data.wast:188	constant expression required
data.wast:195	constant expression required
data.wast:89	unknown index
data.wast:90	unknown index
